          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods, including hybrid retrieval that merges sparse and dense search.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Single authoritative source; addresses core retrieval patterns with practical validation (production deployment patterns). Awaits additional independent corroboration.\\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets, often via reciprocal rank fusion (RRF), to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\nThis approach is particularly effective for queries that mix rare identifiers\u2014such as error codes and product SKUs\u2014with natural language intent. By leveraging both exact lexical matching and semantic understanding, hybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone on heterogeneous query workloads.\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems, reflecting its robustness and practical effectiveness across diverse real-world scenarios.\\n\\n## Reciprocal Rank Fusion\\n\\nUse reciprocal rank fusion for robust result merging. RRF combines rankings from multiple retrievers by computing a normalized score for each result based on its position in each ranked list, eliminating the need to harmonize raw scores across disparate retrieval systems.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-03\\n\u0022}"
        }
